                 one="A perceptron sorts points by which side of a straight line they fall on, and nudges the line whenever it's wrong.",
                 bridge=('A credit-score rule', "Add points for each fact (income +30, missed payment −50) and approve if the total clears a cutoff. That's a perceptron: the weights are the points and the bias moves the cutoff. Learning means adjusting the points each time it approves someone it shouldn't have, or rejects someone it should have approved."),
                 biz="The perceptron was funded by the US Office of Naval Research. Military money paid for most early AI, and the promises made to it set expectations the technology couldn't meet for decades.",
                 body="<p>It computes a weighted sum of the inputs plus a [[bias]] and thresholds it. Geometrically, <em>w·x + b = 0</em> is a line, so the perceptron is a [[linear classifier]]. Rosenblatt's learning rule moves the line toward every point it misclassifies.</p>",
                 tex=r'\hat y = \mathrm{step}(w\cdot x + b) \qquad w \leftarrow w + \eta\,(y-\hat y)\,x',
                 era="Rosenblatt's Mark I Perceptron was room-sized hardware with motor-driven potentiometers for weights and a 20×20 grid of photocells for eyes. Learning meant motors physically turning knobs.",
