                 bridge=('A safety buffer and a derived column', '<strong>Margin:</strong> of all the lines that separate the classes, pick the one with the biggest buffer on both sides, like routing a road as far as possible from both cliff edges.</p><p><strong>Kernel:</strong> if no rule works on your columns, add a computed one. Points inside a ring vs outside can\'t be split by a line on (x, y), but add <code>r = x² + y²</code> and "r &lt; 1" is a one-line rule. The kernel trick gets that effect without ever building the new columns, even infinitely many of them.'),
                 biz='SVMs ran much of 2000s industry ML (text classification, bioinformatics, early vision): reliable, few knobs, good open-source libraries like libsvm. Predictability sold better than peak accuracy.',
                 body="<p>Only the points on the curbs, the [[support vectors]], define the [[margin]]. The [[kernel trick]] separates data no line can split by implicitly lifting it into more dimensions. The film's version: lift by x₁² + x₂², then cut with a flat blade. In the lab, the HOG features from §1.4 go into a linear SVM on real photos: the 2005 recipe, and the baseline the CNN has to beat next.</p>",
